data.reasoningText:
Now I have a good understanding of the project. Let me plan and implement the components.

Component tree:
```
ApplyPage                    (page — owns all form state, orchestrates steps)
├── StepIndicator            (shows step progress bar)
├── PersonalInfoStep         (step 1)
├── WorkHistoryStep          (step 2)
│   └── WorkEntryForm        (single work entry)
├── ReferencesStep           (step 3)
│   └── ReferenceForm        (single reference)
├── CoverLetterStep          (step 4)
└── ReviewStep               (step 5)
```

I need to set up the models, service interface, and all the components. I'll start by creating the job application models and service interface, then build out each component in a dedicated subfolder, and finally register everything in the service container.

The structure will be:
- Models/JobApplicationModels.cs for the data records and service interface
- Services/ApplicationService.cs as a stub since the actual implementation is already registered in DI
- Components/Pages/Apply/ directory containing the main page and all sub-components

Let me start implementing these files.

```

csharp
// Models/JobApplicationModels.cs
namespace JobApplicationApp.Models;

public record PersonalInfo(string FirstName, string LastName, string Email, string Phone);

public record WorkEntry(string Company, string Position, DateTime StartDate, DateTime EndDate, string Description);

public record Reference(string Name, string Title, string Company, string Email, string Phone);

public record JobApplication(
    PersonalInfo PersonalInfo,
    List<WorkEntry> WorkHistory,
    List<Reference> References,
    string CoverLetter
);

public interface IApplicationService
{
    Task<bool> SubmitApplicationAsync(JobApplication application);
}
```

Now I'll create the Blazor components starting with the main ApplyPage and its child components.

```razor
// Components/Pages/Apply/ApplyPage.razor
@page "/apply"
@using JobApplicationApp.Models
@inject IApplicationService ApplicationService

<div class="apply-container">
    <h1>Job Application</h1>

    <StepIndicator CurrentStep="currentStep" TotalSteps="4" />

    <div class="form-content">
        @switch(currentStep)
        {
            case 1:
                <PersonalInfoStep @bind-Data="application.PersonalInfo" />
                break;
            case 2:
                <WorkHistoryStep @bind-Data="application.WorkHistory" />
                break;
            case 3:
                <ReferencesStep @bind-Data="application.References" />
                break;
            case 4:
                <CoverLetterStep @bind-Data="application.CoverLetter" />
                break;
            case 5:
                <ReviewStep Data="application" />
                break;
        }
    </div>

    <div class="button-group">
        @if(currentStep > 1)
        {
            <button @onclick="PreviousStep">Previous</button>
        }
        @if(currentStep < 5)
        {
            <button @onclick="NextStep">Next</button>
        }
        @if(currentStep == 5)
        {
            <button @onclick="Submit">Submit Application</button>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private int currentStep = 1;
    private JobApplication application = new(
        new PersonalInfo("", "", "", ""),
        new List<WorkEntry>(),
        new List<Reference>(),
        ""
    );

    private void NextStep() => currentStep++;
    private void PreviousStep() => currentStep--;

    private async Task Submit()
    {
        await ApplicationService.SubmitApplicationAsync(application);
    }
}
```

Now creating the step components and supporting forms.

```razor
// Components/Pages/Apply/StepIndicator.razor
@using System.Collections.Generic

<div class="step-indicator">
    @for(int i = 1; i <= TotalSteps; i++)
    {
        <div class="step @(i == CurrentStep ? "active" : "") @(i < CurrentStep ? "completed" : "")">
            <span>@i</span>
        </div>
        @if(i < TotalSteps)
        {
            <div class="step-line"></div>
        }
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter]
    public int CurrentStep { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public int TotalSteps { get; set; }
}
```

Building the form components... Defining the reference model class...

I need to decide whether each step component should manage its own EditForm or if the parent page should handle it. Since each step has distinct validation rules, I'll give each step its own form. Now I'm planning the implementation in batches: first the models and leaf components like the work entry and reference forms, then the step components themselves, and finally the main apply page with styling and dependency injection setup.

For the directory structure, I'll organize models in a dedicated folder and keep all the Razor components under Components/Pages/Apply. I'm planning to use Bootstrap for the base styling since it's already available, then add CSS isolation for custom styling on the step indicator and other step-specific elements.

Now I'm thinking through the architecture for the step components. The parent page should own the IApplicationService and handle the async operations like email validation, while each step component manages its own form state and local validation. I'll have the step components call back to the parent through callbacks for operations that need the service, keeping the separation of concerns clean.

Let me organize the code structure now. I'll create a models file for all the form classes and then build out the components in their own folder.

Defining the mutable form models... Adding data validation attributes...

I need to mark the required fields with validation annotations—PersonalInfo fields are all required, WorkEntry needs Company, Title, and StartDate, Reference requires Name, Relationship, and Email, and CoverLetter has a 2000 character limit. For the multi-step wizard, I'll use EditForm with DataAnnotationsValidator on each step so validation only passes when the user can proceed to the next section. Now I'm thinking through the styling for the step indicator.

I'm building a progress bar component with Bootstrap and CSS isolation to show which step the user is on. My plan is to create the models file, then build out each step component—PersonalInfoStep, WorkHistoryStep with a sub-component for individual work entries, ReferencesStep with its own reference form sub-component, and CoverLetterStep. I'll also need a StepIndicator component for the progress bar and a Review step to wrap everything together.

Now I'm ready to start implementing. I'll create the directory structure first, then build out all the components in batches, relying on Bootstrap for styling and custom CSS for the wizard-specific look.

Defining the data models... Adding more form models... Building the step indicator markup... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Writing form markup... Writing the form layout... Writing form fields... Writing the form component... Finishing the RemoveEntry method to prevent removing the last entry, then realizing that ObjectGraphDataAnnotationsValidator isn't available in newer .NET versions and the standard DataAnnotationsValidator only validates top-level models. I need to rethink the validation approach for validating multiple work entries at once—either using manual validation or wrapping the entries in a container model. I'm going back and forth on whether to wrap the list in a validatable model or just do manual validation in the handler. The simplest path forward is to keep PersonalInfoStep using EditForm with DataAnnotationsValidator, then handle WorkHistoryStep and ReferencesStep with manual validation that checks required fields and displays errors inline. with the simplest approach: EditForm with DataAnnotationsValidator for PersonalInfoStep since it's flat, then regular forms with manual validation for WorkHistoryStep and ReferencesStep, and a basic textarea for CoverLetterStep. I could wrap each work entry in its own EditForm, but that's overcomplicating things—I'll just validate everything manually in the submit handler and use a ValidationMessageStore if I need to display errors.

Actually, plain HTML forms with Bootstrap validation classes are cleaner here. I'm building out the work history step with a loop that renders each entry, displays any validation errors in an alert, and includes buttons to add or remove positions.

Writing component markup... Finishing the validation logic to check all required fields, then invoking the next callback. For the form inputs, I'll use regular HTML input elements with data binding since they're not wrapped in an EditForm, which would be required for the built-in input components. Let me map out the approach for each step component. I need to clarify the approach: PersonalInfoStep will use EditForm with InputText components and validation, while WorkEntryForm and ReferenceForm will use regular HTML input elements with @bind for manual validation. Let me write out all the component files now. Adding the namespace import...

Now I'm ready to generate all the component and model files. I'll start with the models, then build out each step component along with the main Apply page and its styling. Writing component structure... Adding the form submission logic...

Now I'm thinking through how to handle the cover letter binding in the child components. Since it's a string value type, I can't just pass it by reference like the other data models. I should use an `EventCallback<string>` to let the CoverLetterStep component notify the parent when the value changes, or wrap it in a simple class to make it a reference type that the component can modify directly. I should use the input event handler approach instead of @bind on the textarea, since that lets me properly invoke the callback without the child directly mutating the parameter. This way the parent stays in control of the state update. Writing the review step... Writing component markup...

Now I have the full picture of the application form layout. I'm ready to set up the project structure and create all the necessary files.

